description:
On every command, Valkey's call() does work for features that are out of rvk's declared scope (FEATURE_MATRIX §8 lists commandstats, LATENCY and COMMANDLOG as out of scope). The work is:
- extra time reads (server.c:4201-4214, 4263-4269);
- commandstats calls/usec (server.c:4339-4340);
- the per-command HDR latency histogram, hdr_record_value (server.c:4341-4342 -> 3978-3985). latency-tracking defaults to on (config.c:3488) and the harness does not turn it off (bench/harness.py:110-112);
- durationAddSample (server.c:4313-4318);
- three commandlog threshold checks (commandlog.c:150-167);
- a MONITOR feed check (server.c:4330-4334);
- a peak-memory sample through zmalloc_used_memory() (server.c:4413-4414).
beforeSleep() adds about 5 monotonic clock reads plus duration and peak samples on every event-loop pass (server.c:1946-1947, 2005-2123).

rvk's run_command does one relaxed counter store (cmd.rs:382) and one now_ms() (cmd.rs:431). That clock read is needed anyway for expiry.

Estimated cost: on this host the clocksource is tsc and constant_tsc is set, so Valkey uses rdtsc and the clock reads are cheap. That leaves about 30-60 ns per command, mostly the histogram and stat updates, plus about 0.3-1 us per event-loop pass spread over that pass's commands.
- Non-pipelined (3-10 us per op): about 0.5-1.5%.
- Pipeline 16 (about 0.7-1.5 us per op): about 3-8%.
On hosts whose clocksource is not tsc (kvm-clock, xen), ustime and getMonotonicUs fall back to clock_gettime. Valkey then makes 2-3 clock calls per command against rvk's 1, and the gap grows.

This is operational overhead, not something Valkey compatibility in the declared scope requires. It is the largest systematic item that biases the comparison toward rvk.

For the net direction of the comparison, rvk also has per-op costs that Valkey lacks:
- a Mutex lock and unlock per keyed command (store.rs:112-114);
- finish() stores the shard's mem counter on every keyed command, GETs included, even when it is unchanged (cmd.rs:437-451, store.rs:118-120). The 64 counters sit in one contiguous 512-byte array, 8 per cache line (store.rs:77), so with 2 or more worker threads most stores take a cross-core coherence miss;
- with maxmemory set, make_room reads all 8 of those lines on every DENYOOM command (cmd.rs:518, store.rs:123-125);
- a heap-allocated argv Vec per command (cmd.rs:351), plus a clone in cmd_set (cmd.rs:815);
- each key is hashed twice (cmd.rs:461, cmd.rs:279);
- a 16 KB zero-filled rbuf is allocated per connection (server.rs:291);
- there is no batch key prefetch, which Valkey has (networking.c:4683).
These understate rvk, mainly in rvk-2t and pipelined runs. Any statement about bias should net them against the Valkey-side items rather than list only one side.

Checked so that the write-up does not attribute rvk's lead to these. Each costs a few ns or less on Valkey's GET/SET path with default config, or rvk does the same work.

Gated by a flag or list check in Valkey (out of rvk's scope):
- keyspace notifications: off by default; returns after the module-subscriber and flag checks (notify.c:125-132);
- tracking invalidation: TrackingTable==NULL (tracking.c:373-374);
- client output-buffer limits: only checked once the reply list is in use (networking.c:6786-6795); the normal class defaults to 0 0 0 (config.c:204-205);
- client memory buckets and evictClients: maxmemory-clients 0 (server.c:1153-1160, networking.c:7141);
- latency monitor: threshold 0 (latency.h:93-94);
- commandlog: 3 compares (commandlog.c:150-167);
- hot keys and throttling: disabled (hotkeys.c:117-122, throttle.c:303-309);
- propagation with no AOF or replicas (server.c:3787-3799);
- ACL check for the default all-permissions user.

Work both servers do (parity):
- WATCH touch: dictSize==0 (multi.c:598) vs watched.is_empty() (store.rs:362-365);
- LRU touch on every access (db.c:117-121 vs store.rs:183-187);
- keyspace hits/misses (db.c:123-127 vs cmd.rs:311-317);
- lazy-expire check (db.c:2213-2216 vs store.rs:170-179);
- per-read query-buffer limit check (networking.c:4800-4804 vs server.rs:330-332);
- the approximate-LRU eviction pool, similar work per evicted key (evict.c:486-592 vs cmd.rs:544-589).

Together, the gated items add up to roughly 20-30 ns per op or less. The syscall pattern is also matched: one read per readiness event, and one write per batch of replies.
